and it can help us to stay on track.

489
00:39:31,423 --> 00:39:34,025
So we should word the issue in neutral terms

490
00:39:34,775 --> 00:39:35,415
For example,

491
00:39:36,135 --> 00:39:36,376
should

492
00:39:37,436 --> 00:39:41,557
the USA have a stricter gun control laws

493
00:39:42,457 --> 00:39:48,479
and not should the government be doing more to keep guns out of the hands of criminals?

494
00:39:49,039 --> 00:39:49,359
Okay.

495
00:39:50,440 --> 00:39:51,100
So the more

496
00:39:51,900 --> 00:39:57,222
the issue is clear, so the better it will be kind of to debate the argument.

497
00:39:57,482 --> 00:39:57,782
Okay.

498
00:39:58,602 --> 00:39:59,482
And secondly,

499
00:40:00,803 --> 00:40:01,183
develop

500
00:40:02,527 --> 00:40:05,430
a list of premises that address the issue.

501
00:40:08,232 --> 00:40:14,937
So in coming up with a list of possible premises, we should kind of put our

502
00:40:15,018 --> 00:40:20,782
personal opinions aside and we should avoid the trap of seeing the issue as having two sides,

503
00:40:21,343 --> 00:40:27,979
one right and one wrong. So the two sides approach tends to be the norm in some culture in

504
00:40:28,059 --> 00:40:30,241
which rhetoric is used to settle issues

505
00:40:31,141 --> 00:40:35,004
with one side winning and the other side losing.

506
00:40:35,685 --> 00:40:40,149
And in developing a list of premises, we should try to remain as objective

507
00:40:41,770 --> 00:40:43,471
and open-minded as possible.

508
00:40:44,112 --> 00:40:49,656
And rather than selecting only those premises that support our particular worldview, we should

509
00:40:49,716 --> 00:40:53,143
try to explore explore all sides of the issue

510
00:40:54,003 --> 00:41:00,807
and we should brainstorm with others and letting the ideas flow freely and creatively.

511
00:41:01,527 --> 00:41:04,089
It can help us to widen our perspective.

512
00:41:04,289 --> 00:41:04,629
Okay.

513
00:41:08,071 --> 00:41:11,072
And our premises should be relatively uncontroversial.

514
00:41:11,232 --> 00:41:11,572
Okay.

515
00:41:12,353 --> 00:41:16,735
And we should watch out for unsupported interpretations or assumptions,

516
00:41:17,515 --> 00:41:20,077
and we can also adopt the skeptics attitude.

517
00:41:20,097 --> 00:41:20,717
Okay.

518
00:41:22,563 --> 00:41:23,924
And in doing our research,

519
00:41:24,805 --> 00:41:25,865
we should make sure we

520
00:41:26,606 --> 00:41:31,950
use only reliable sources and continue to consider all sides of the issue.

521
00:41:41,356 --> 00:41:43,577
And the next one is to eliminate

522
00:41:44,458 --> 00:41:46,539
weak or irrelevant premises.

523
00:41:47,300 --> 00:41:47,680
Okay?

524
00:41:48,421 --> 00:41:53,994
So after coming up with our list of premises, and we should review them once again,

525
00:41:54,775 --> 00:42:01,499
eliminate any premises that are weak or irrelevant to the particular problem or issue.

526
00:42:08,004 --> 00:42:10,085
And we should also resist the temptation

527
00:42:10,825 --> 00:42:12,166
to eliminate premises

528
00:42:12,907 --> 00:42:17,610
that don't mesh with our particular opinion regarding the issue.

529
00:42:35,008 --> 00:42:38,391
And the next one is we should establish a conclusion.

530
00:42:39,132 --> 00:42:45,597
So only after we are satisfied with our list of premises, then we should draw a

531
00:42:45,657 --> 00:42:48,820
particular conclusion, a particular point or thesis.

532
00:42:49,180 --> 00:42:49,540
Okay.

533
00:42:50,881 --> 00:42:54,564
So we should examine all the premises we have retained

534
00:42:55,265 --> 00:43:01,450
and in developing a conclusion, we should keep an open mind and we can ask ourselves what conclusion

535
00:43:01,911 --> 00:43:03,612
follows from these premises.

536
00:43:05,123 --> 00:43:10,644
And remember to avoid looking at our issue as a contest between two sides.

537
00:43:11,405 --> 00:43:18,346
And we can look at all the premises in our final list and consider how the conclusion can take

538
00:43:18,526 --> 00:43:21,267
into account as many of the premises as possible.

539
00:43:28,109 --> 00:43:30,730
And next one, we should organize our argument.

540
00:43:31,290 --> 00:43:32,250
So there are many

541
00:43:33,036 --> 00:43:35,858
and ways to organize an argument.

542
00:43:35,898 --> 00:43:42,861
For example, we can first list or diagram our premises and conclusion, or we can present our

543
00:43:42,961 --> 00:43:45,083
argument in the written or oral form.

544
00:43:45,723 --> 00:43:52,166
So if we are presenting our argument in essay form or making an oral presentation, we should

545
00:43:52,226 --> 00:43:58,730
clearly state the issue in the first paragraph of our essay or in our opening sentence.

546
00:43:59,270 --> 00:44:06,256
So this will allow our audience to easily identify exactly what issue we are addressing.

547
00:44:08,758 --> 00:44:14,403
And the conclusion also usually appears in the first paragraph or at the beginning of the presentation.

548
00:44:15,144 --> 00:44:17,526
And in the essays, that's sometimes called the

549
00:44:18,406 --> 00:44:20,949
thesis statement, okay, like I was mentioning.

550
00:44:21,589 --> 00:44:26,153
And if possible, we should limit our thesis statement to one sentence.

551
00:44:27,750 --> 00:44:33,973
And our opening paragraph can also let the reader know how we plan to defend our conclusion

552
00:44:34,153 --> 00:44:36,054
and organize our argument.

553
00:44:37,615 --> 00:44:43,518
As well as include a sentence or two to grab the reader's attention about the issue's importance.

554
00:44:50,421 --> 00:44:54,403
And the next one is to try out your argument on others.

555
00:44:54,984 --> 00:45:01,327
So once we have come up with what we think is a strong argument, we are ready to try it out

556
00:45:01,948 --> 00:45:07,070
on someone else. And in doing this, we should keep in mind that as critical thinkers,

557
00:45:07,831 --> 00:45:11,893
we need to be both open-minded and good listeners.

558
00:45:12,901 --> 00:45:19,625
If we find that our argument is weak or that the particular conclusion we have drawn does

559
00:45:19,705 --> 00:45:25,308
not follow from the premises, we should go back and revise our argument.

560
00:45:25,748 --> 00:45:26,108
Okay?

561
00:45:27,669 --> 00:45:32,391
And the next logical step is to revise our argument.

562
00:45:33,072 --> 00:45:33,432
So,

563
00:45:34,818 --> 00:45:37,799
If necessary, we should revise our argument

564
00:45:38,739 --> 00:45:41,820
when we receive particular feedback.

565
00:45:42,500 --> 00:45:48,101
And if the other person's counter argument is compelling, it would be irrational

566
00:45:49,001 --> 00:45:55,363
for you not to accept it and revise your own position in light of the counter argument.

567
00:46:02,242 --> 00:46:02,382
And

568
00:46:03,123 --> 00:46:04,904
the last one is

569
00:46:06,065 --> 00:46:09,307
putting our solution or conclusion into action.

570
00:46:09,748 --> 00:46:10,708
So if necessary.

571
00:46:11,449 --> 00:46:11,829
So

572
00:46:12,690 --> 00:46:18,073
sometimes if it's appropriate, we should put our solution or conclusion into action.

573
00:46:18,774 --> 00:46:22,417
So good critical thinking has a behavioral component.

574
00:46:23,337 --> 00:46:25,839
It involves taking critical action.

575
00:46:26,519 --> 00:46:28,681
So for instance, if you're writing to

576
00:46:30,771 --> 00:46:32,772
for example, to the government, right?

577
00:46:33,153 --> 00:46:34,334
Or to some politicians,

578
00:46:35,094 --> 00:46:35,955
to some

579
00:46:37,796 --> 00:46:40,758
maybe officials, let's say government officials, right?

580
00:46:41,298 --> 00:46:41,939
About

581
00:46:43,280 --> 00:46:45,101
certain particular issue, right?

582
00:46:45,701 --> 00:46:46,942
So you may want

583
00:46:47,663 --> 00:46:49,704
to suggest a realistic solution

584
00:46:50,425 --> 00:46:54,968
to that problem and also offer to help with this implementation.

585
00:46:56,409 --> 00:47:03,336
And knowing how to construct and present an argument are important skills for critical thinker.

586
00:47:03,916 --> 00:47:04,916
So it's not only

587
00:47:05,716 --> 00:47:07,497
makes you more effective

588
00:47:08,697 --> 00:47:10,898
in presenting an argument or an issue,

589
00:47:12,158 --> 00:47:17,620
but can also help us in resolving issues in our own lives also.

590
00:47:21,401 --> 00:47:22,461
So with these

591
00:47:23,502 --> 00:47:24,482
ideas in mind,

592
00:47:25,222 --> 00:47:27,523
let me finish our today's topic.

593
00:47:28,172 --> 00:47:29,640
And thank you very much, guys.

594
00:47:30,384 --> 00:47:31,731
And see you next week.
